{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37\tduplicates.\n38\t\n39\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44\t\n45\t## Use\n46\t\n47\t```sh\n48\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n49\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n50\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n51\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n52\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n53\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n54\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n55\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## Sharing (transport)\n59\t\n60\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n61\t\n62\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n63\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n64\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n65\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n66\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n67\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n68\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n69\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n70\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n71\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n72\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n73\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n74\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n75\t  explicitly either way.\n76\t\n77\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n78\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n79\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n80\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n81\t\n82\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n83\t\n84\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n85\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n86\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n87\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n88\t\n89\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n90\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n91\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n92\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n93\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n94\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n95\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n96\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n97\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n98\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n99\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n100\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n101\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n102\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n103\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n104\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n105\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n106\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n107\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n108\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n109\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n110\t  turns them off.\n111\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n112\t  working either way.\n113\t\n114\t## Adapters\n115\t\n116\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n117\t\n118\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n121\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n122\t\n123\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n124\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n125\tsystems):\n126\t\n127\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n128\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n129\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n130\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n131\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n132\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n133\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n134\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n135\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n136\t\n137\t## Security & redaction\n138\t\n139\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n140\t\n141\t### Redaction layers\n142\t\n143\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n144\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n145\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n146\t\n147\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n148\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n149\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n150\t\n151\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n152\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n153\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n154\t\n155\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n156\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n157\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n158\t\n159\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n160\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n161\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n162\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n163\t\n164\t### Commands\n165\t\n166\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n167\t\n168\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n169\t\n170\t### Maximum safety recipe\n171\t\n172\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n173\t\n174\t## Roadmap\n175\t\n176\t- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n177\t  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n178\t  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n179\t  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n180\t  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n181\t  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n182\t  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n183\t  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n184\t  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n185\t  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n186\t  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n187\t  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n188\t  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n189\t  allowlist for itself.\n190\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n191\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n192\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n193\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n194\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n195\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n196\t  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n197\t  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n198\t  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n199\t  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n200\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n201\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n202\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n203\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n204\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n205\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n206\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n207\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n208\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n209\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n210\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n211\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n212\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n213\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n214\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n215\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n216\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n217\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n218\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n219\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n220\t  scope and capture triggers.\n221\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n222\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n223\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n224\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n225\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n226\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n227\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n228\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n229\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n230\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n231\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n232\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n233\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n234\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n235\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n236\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n237\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n238\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n239\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n240\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n241\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n242\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n243\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n244\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n245\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n246\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n247\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n248\t  intended narrow form.\n249\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n250\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n251\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n252\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n253\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n254\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n255\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n256\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n257\t- **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n258\t  0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n259\t  that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n260\t  blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n261\t  provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n262\t  with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n263\t  *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n264\t  the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n265\t  permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n266\t  conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n267\t  codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n268\t  cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n269\t  blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n270\t  versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n271\t  accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n272\t  `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n273\t  `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n274\t  Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n275\t  carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n276\t  `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n277\t  to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n278\t  reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n279\t  reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n280\t  out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n281\t  never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n282\t  both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n283\t  high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n284\t  it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n285\t  other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n286\t  content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n287\t  reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. *Remaining\n288\t  open questions, mostly turnbridge's to answer at replay time:* blob TTL and\n289\t  API-platform-org auth parity. *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n290\t  `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n291\t  blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n292\t  `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n293\t  rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n294\t  shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope:\n295\t  no ledger event records which model/provider/CLI-version produced a given\n296\t  turn (the `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carrying that are still\n297\t  dropped with no trace) — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n298\t  reasoning, left for a separate roadmap item.\n299\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n300\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n301\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n302\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n303\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n304\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n305\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n306\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n307\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n308\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n309\t  piece.\n310\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n311\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n312\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n313\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n314\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n315\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n316\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n317\t\n318\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n319\t\n320\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n321\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n322\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n323\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n324\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n325\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n326\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n327\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n328\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n329\t\n330\t## License\n331\t\n332\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n333\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QzFWt8UkduSmVwjok9uuad","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"a61d7e48f18594b62ae378267837151b1827b246","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","seq":37},"id":"ev1-d4d4263d1b8d99a77a56d9ae4b613ac8e7f2b44d91db8b5ad4c5f3372c783053","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-24T05:01:21.533Z","producer":{"session_id":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.10.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11\tsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12\tride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13\tpre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14\t\n15\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16\t\n17\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20\t\n21\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t```sh\n27\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37\tduplicates.\n38\t\n39\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44\t\n45\t## Use\n46\t\n47\t```sh\n48\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n49\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n50\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n51\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n52\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n53\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n54\tcledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n55\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## Sharing (transport)\n59\t\n60\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n61\t\n62\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n63\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n64\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n65\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n66\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n67\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n68\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n69\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n70\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n71\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n72\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n73\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n74\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n75\t  explicitly either way.\n76\t\n77\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n78\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n79\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n80\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n81\t\n82\t## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n83\t\n84\tEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n85\tmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n86\tbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n87\tthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n88\t\n89\t- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n90\t  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n91\t  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n92\t  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n93\t  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n94\t  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n95\t  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n96\t  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n97\t- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n98\t  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n99\t  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n100\t  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n101\t  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n102\t  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n103\t  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n104\t  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n105\t  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n106\t  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n107\t  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n108\t  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n109\t  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n110\t  turns them off.\n111\t- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n112\t  working either way.\n113\t\n114\t## Adapters\n115\t\n116\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n117\t\n118\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n121\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n122\t\n123\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n124\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n125\tsystems):\n126\t\n127\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n128\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n129\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n130\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n131\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n132\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n133\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n134\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n135\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n136\t\n137\t## Security & redaction\n138\t\n139\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n140\t\n141\t### Redaction layers\n142\t\n143\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n144\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n145\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n146\t\n147\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n148\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n149\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n150\t\n151\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n152\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n153\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n154\t\n155\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n156\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n157\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n158\t\n159\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n160\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n161\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n162\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n163\t\n164\t### Commands\n165\t\n166\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n167\t\n168\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n169\t\n170\t### Maximum safety recipe\n171\t\n172\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n173\t\n174\t## Roadmap\n175\t\n176\t- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n177\t  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n178\t  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n179\t  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n180\t  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n181\t  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n182\t  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n183\t  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n184\t  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n185\t  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n186\t  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n187\t  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n188\t  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n189\t  allowlist for itself.\n190\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n191\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n192\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n193\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n194\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n195\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n196\t  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n197\t  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n198\t  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n199\t  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n200\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n201\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n202\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n203\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n204\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n205\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n206\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n207\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n208\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n209\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n210\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n211\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n212\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n213\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n214\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n215\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n216\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n217\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n218\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n219\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n220\t  scope and capture triggers.\n221\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n222\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n223\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n224\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n225\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n226\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n227\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n228\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n229\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n230\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n231\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n232\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n233\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n234\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n235\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n236\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n237\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n238\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n239\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n240\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n241\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n242\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n243\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n244\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n245\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n246\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n247\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n248\t  intended narrow form.\n249\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n250\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n251\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n252\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n253\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n254\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n255\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n256\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n257\t- **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n258\t  0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n259\t  that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n260\t  blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n261\t  provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n262\t  with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n263\t  *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n264\t  the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n265\t  permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n266\t  conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n267\t  codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n268\t  cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n269\t  blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n270\t  versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n271\t  accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n272\t  `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n273\t  `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n274\t  Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n275\t  carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n276\t  `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n277\t  to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n278\t  reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n279\t  reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n280\t  out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n281\t  never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n282\t  both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n283\t  high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n284\t  it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n285\t  other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n286\t  content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n287\t  reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. *Remaining\n288\t  open questions, mostly turnbridge's to answer at replay time:* blob TTL and\n289\t  API-platform-org auth parity. *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n290\t  `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n291\t  blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n292\t  `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n293\t  rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n294\t  shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope:\n295\t  no ledger event records which model/provider/CLI-version produced a given\n296\t  turn (the `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carrying that are still\n297\t  dropped with no trace) — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n298\t  reasoning, left for a separate roadmap item.\n299\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n300\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n301\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n302\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n303\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n304\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n305\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n306\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n307\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n308\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n309\t  piece.\n310\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n311\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n312\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n313\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n314\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n315\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n316\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n317\t\n318\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n319\t\n320\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n321\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n322\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n323\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n324\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n325\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n326\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n327\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n328\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n329\t\n330\t## License\n331\t\n332\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n333\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QzFWt8UkduSmVwjok9uuad","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"29110d51-aa9f-4e92-abc4-3e1dcab5998c","promptId":"c1b4f319-a8fa-4bf4-a300-2234b56ef2c1","sessionId":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","session_id":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","slug":"vectorized-hopping-possum","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"29110d51-aa9f-4e92-abc4-3e1dcab5998c","timestamp":"2026-07-24T05:01:21.533Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Conversation Ledger\n\nDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\nyour repository as git notes.\n\n`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\nutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\nconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\nconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\nsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\nride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\npre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n\nConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n\n- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n\nThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\nvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\ncd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\ncledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n```\n\n`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\nand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\nbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\nrepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\nidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\nduplicates.\n\nTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\ncapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\ninteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\ncodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n\n## Use\n\n```sh\ncledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\ncledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\ncledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\ncledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\ncledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\ncledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\ncledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\necho '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n```\n\n## Sharing (transport)\n\nThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n\n- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n  explicitly either way.\n\nIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\nare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\nthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\nand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n\n## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n\nEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\nmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\nbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\nthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n\n- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n  turns them off.\n- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n  working either way.\n\n## Adapters\n\nSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n\n| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n\nTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\nstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\nsystems):\n\n- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n\n## Security & redaction\n\nVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n\n### Redaction layers\n\n**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n\n**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n- Cost: manual configuration required.\n\n**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n\n**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n\n**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n\n### Commands\n\n`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n\n`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n\n### Maximum safety recipe\n\nKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n\n## Roadmap\n\n- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n  allowlist for itself.\n- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n  scope and capture triggers.\n- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n  intended narrow form.\n- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n  capture runs silently on every turn.\n- **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n  0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n  that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n  blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n  provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n  with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n  *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n  the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n  permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n  conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n  codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n  cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n  blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n  versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n  accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n  `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n  `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n  Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n  carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n  `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n  to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n  reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n  reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n  out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n  never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n  both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n  high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n  it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n  other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n  content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n  reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. *Remaining\n  open questions, mostly turnbridge's to answer at replay time:* blob TTL and\n  API-platform-org auth parity. *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n  `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n  blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n  `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n  rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n  shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope:\n  no ledger event records which model/provider/CLI-version produced a given\n  turn (the `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carrying that are still\n  dropped with no trace) — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n  reasoning, left for a separate roadmap item.\n- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n  piece.\n- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n\n## Storage model, in one paragraph\n\nOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\nnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\ncontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\nconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\nrepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\ncapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\nrewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\nfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\ntool deliberately refuses to become.\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md","numLines":333,"startLine":1,"totalLines":333},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"3e238cae-a7e4-4a21-8168-f97bed8a1e87","version":"2.1.218"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-07-24T05:03:11.380Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Product intent\n2\t\n3\t## Purpose\n4\t\n5\tTurnbridge lets a developer continue a visible coding conversation in a\n6\tdifferent agent CLI when they choose to switch providers or tools. It retains\n7\tthe full user-visible turn history—messages, tool calls, and tool results—\n8\trather than requiring a last-minute summary from a rate-limited model.\n9\t\n10\tIt serves two users who are often the same person on different days: a solo\n11\tdeveloper hopping CLIs mid-task (rate limits, model choice), and small\n12\tcollaborating groups whose members use *different* CLIs and want to pick up\n13\teach other's conversations. Sharing rides on Conversation Ledger's git-notes\n14\tstorage; each turn is attributed to its human author via the repo's git\n15\tidentity, and listings show only your own (plus unattributed) conversations\n16\tunless you ask for everyone's.\n17\t\n18\t## Behavioral commitments\n19\t\n20\t- Capture every available visible turn incrementally, not only on clean exit\n21\t  (delivered by Conversation Ledger's hooks; see division below).\n22\t- Preserve source content faithfully and label its originating CLI.\n23\t- Offer one resume picker across supported CLIs while making the session type\n24\t  honest: native resume for same-CLI records, native-format fabrication for\n25\t  cross-CLI records, transcript rehydration as the always-available fallback.\n26\t- Prefer conversations whose repository and code state match the current work;\n27\t  warn rather than silently treating mismatched history as current.\n28\t- Never claim to transfer hidden reasoning, private provider state, or an\n29\t  exact native session; a fabricated session is labeled as imported.\n30\t- Put the developer in control of capture, sharing, and deletion. Sharing is\n31\t  a deliberate choice, not a side effect: today it is an explicit\n32\t  `cledger sync`; the decided direction (see Conversation Ledger's design\n33\t  doc) is optional-but-default-on transport hooks so records ride normal\n34\t  `git push`/`pull`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n35\t  user. Listings default to the current user's records.\n36\t\n37\t## Non-goals\n38\t\n39\t- A team presence system, task tracker, or agent orchestration framework.\n40\t- Inferring or replacing human intent.\n41\t- Summarized \"handoff notes\" (sticky-note-style); Turnbridge moves the literal\n42\t  record.\n43\t\n44\t## Relationship to Conversation Ledger\n45\t\n46\tTurnbridge is a client of Conversation Ledger, which owns the durable neutral\n47\trecord **and** the source-CLI capture adapters (hooks, transcript parsers,\n48\tcursors, dedup). Turnbridge owns everything on the resume side: the unified\n49\tpicker, ownership/compatibility filtering, target-CLI session fabrication,\n50\tbootstrap rehydration, and the optional command shims.\n51\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kxky1jty3jWGjQQDvkBTXE","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"a61d7e48f18594b62ae378267837151b1827b246","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","seq":52},"id":"ev1-df7f6f49c2768d5d6604bbb44415eacaf68f19757c0d9e42a43bba2626611d87","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-24T05:01:34.392Z","producer":{"session_id":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.10.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Product intent\n2\t\n3\t## Purpose\n4\t\n5\tTurnbridge lets a developer continue a visible coding conversation in a\n6\tdifferent agent CLI when they choose to switch providers or tools. It retains\n7\tthe full user-visible turn history—messages, tool calls, and tool results—\n8\trather than requiring a last-minute summary from a rate-limited model.\n9\t\n10\tIt serves two users who are often the same person on different days: a solo\n11\tdeveloper hopping CLIs mid-task (rate limits, model choice), and small\n12\tcollaborating groups whose members use *different* CLIs and want to pick up\n13\teach other's conversations. Sharing rides on Conversation Ledger's git-notes\n14\tstorage; each turn is attributed to its human author via the repo's git\n15\tidentity, and listings show only your own (plus unattributed) conversations\n16\tunless you ask for everyone's.\n17\t\n18\t## Behavioral commitments\n19\t\n20\t- Capture every available visible turn incrementally, not only on clean exit\n21\t  (delivered by Conversation Ledger's hooks; see division below).\n22\t- Preserve source content faithfully and label its originating CLI.\n23\t- Offer one resume picker across supported CLIs while making the session type\n24\t  honest: native resume for same-CLI records, native-format fabrication for\n25\t  cross-CLI records, transcript rehydration as the always-available fallback.\n26\t- Prefer conversations whose repository and code state match the current work;\n27\t  warn rather than silently treating mismatched history as current.\n28\t- Never claim to transfer hidden reasoning, private provider state, or an\n29\t  exact native session; a fabricated session is labeled as imported.\n30\t- Put the developer in control of capture, sharing, and deletion. Sharing is\n31\t  a deliberate choice, not a side effect: today it is an explicit\n32\t  `cledger sync`; the decided direction (see Conversation Ledger's design\n33\t  doc) is optional-but-default-on transport hooks so records ride normal\n34\t  `git push`/`pull`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n35\t  user. Listings default to the current user's records.\n36\t\n37\t## Non-goals\n38\t\n39\t- A team presence system, task tracker, or agent orchestration framework.\n40\t- Inferring or replacing human intent.\n41\t- Summarized \"handoff notes\" (sticky-note-style); Turnbridge moves the literal\n42\t  record.\n43\t\n44\t## Relationship to Conversation Ledger\n45\t\n46\tTurnbridge is a client of Conversation Ledger, which owns the durable neutral\n47\trecord **and** the source-CLI capture adapters (hooks, transcript parsers,\n48\tcursors, dedup). Turnbridge owns everything on the resume side: the unified\n49\tpicker, ownership/compatibility filtering, target-CLI session fabrication,\n50\tbootstrap rehydration, and the optional command shims.\n51\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kxky1jty3jWGjQQDvkBTXE","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"a884e9ac-e07a-412e-aafe-f296abd9659f","promptId":"c1b4f319-a8fa-4bf4-a300-2234b56ef2c1","sessionId":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","session_id":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","slug":"vectorized-hopping-possum","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a884e9ac-e07a-412e-aafe-f296abd9659f","timestamp":"2026-07-24T05:01:34.392Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Product intent\n\n## Purpose\n\nTurnbridge lets a developer continue a visible coding conversation in a\ndifferent agent CLI when they choose to switch providers or tools. It retains\nthe full user-visible turn history—messages, tool calls, and tool results—\nrather than requiring a last-minute summary from a rate-limited model.\n\nIt serves two users who are often the same person on different days: a solo\ndeveloper hopping CLIs mid-task (rate limits, model choice), and small\ncollaborating groups whose members use *different* CLIs and want to pick up\neach other's conversations. Sharing rides on Conversation Ledger's git-notes\nstorage; each turn is attributed to its human author via the repo's git\nidentity, and listings show only your own (plus unattributed) conversations\nunless you ask for everyone's.\n\n## Behavioral commitments\n\n- Capture every available visible turn incrementally, not only on clean exit\n  (delivered by Conversation Ledger's hooks; see division below).\n- Preserve source content faithfully and label its originating CLI.\n- Offer one resume picker across supported CLIs while making the session type\n  honest: native resume for same-CLI records, native-format fabrication for\n  cross-CLI records, transcript rehydration as the always-available fallback.\n- Prefer conversations whose repository and code state match the current work;\n  warn rather than silently treating mismatched history as current.\n- Never claim to transfer hidden reasoning, private provider state, or an\n  exact native session; a fabricated session is labeled as imported.\n- Put the developer in control of capture, sharing, and deletion. Sharing is\n  a deliberate choice, not a side effect: today it is an explicit\n  `cledger sync`; the decided direction (see Conversation Ledger's design\n  doc) is optional-but-default-on transport hooks so records ride normal\n  `git push`/`pull`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n  user. Listings default to the current user's records.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- A team presence system, task tracker, or agent orchestration framework.\n- Inferring or replacing human intent.\n- Summarized \"handoff notes\" (sticky-note-style); Turnbridge moves the literal\n  record.\n\n## Relationship to Conversation Ledger\n\nTurnbridge is a client of Conversation Ledger, which owns the durable neutral\nrecord **and** the source-CLI capture adapters (hooks, transcript parsers,\ncursors, dedup). Turnbridge owns everything on the resume side: the unified\npicker, ownership/compatibility filtering, target-CLI session fabrication,\nbootstrap rehydration, and the optional command shims.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"1e1de6e8-3001-4c66-ac89-d1b63ab727a2","version":"2.1.218"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-07-24T05:03:11.381Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** implemented in v0.1; format may still evolve until v1.\n4\t\n5\t## Scope\n6\t\n7\tConversation Ledger defines a canonical append-only event format plus a local,\n8\tgit-backed storage protocol. It preserves exact visible content while staying\n9\tout of the working tree. Transport is on by default and rides normal git use:\n10\ta pre-push hook pushes the ledger ref alongside `git push` (scan-gated), and\n11\ta fetch refspec stages the remote's ref for lazy merge at read time (see\n12\t\"Transport\"); `cledger sync` remains the explicit path and the fallback when\n13\thooks are declined.\n14\t\n15\t## Canonical objects\n16\t\n17\tAn `EvidenceEvent` is immutable and has:\n18\t\n19\t- `id`: `ev1-` + sha256 of the event's *identity subset* — the durable,\n20\t  source-determined fields (`kind`, `occurred_at`, `actor.type/id`,\n21\t  `producer.source/session_id`, `conversation`, `media_type`, `content`,\n22\t  `links`). Volatile provenance (`recorded_at`, `context`, `raw`,\n23\t  `producer.tool/version`) is excluded, so re-scanning the same source\n24\t  material always yields the same id: capture is idempotent by construction,\n25\t  and the id doubles as the content hash that makes mutation detectable.\n26\t- `schema` version (`conversation-ledger/v1`);\n27\t- `kind`: `conversation_turn`, `decision`, `document`, `annotation`,\n28\t  `redaction`, or `supersession` — an open string; unknown kinds are stored\n29\t  verbatim so downstream tools can extend without a schema release;\n30\t- `occurred_at` (from the source) and `recorded_at` (at append) timestamps;\n31\t- `actor` (human/agent/system + identity) and `producer` (capture tool,\n32\t  source system, native session id) provenance; adapters stamp human turns\n33\t  with the identity a commit made right now would be authored under,\n34\t  resolved by git itself (`git -c user.useConfigOnly=true var\n35\t  GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT`) in git's own precedence: `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL` env, then\n36\t  `user.email` config (includeIf and all), then `EMAIL` env — never a\n37\t  hostname/OS-username guess, which churns (DHCP renames) and would churn\n38\t  event ids. Explicit config is the fallback when strict resolution\n39\t  refuses over one missing field (email set, no name anywhere). When git\n40\t  would have to guess the email too, turns stay unattributed;\n41\t  `cledger install` warns. Because `actor.id` is part of the event identity subset, sessions\n42\t  captured before this stamping existed will produce new event ids if fully\n43\t  re-scanned — the per-session cursor normally prevents that, but a forced\n44\t  rescan can duplicate pre-identity turns;\n45\t- `content`, stored inline, with an optional `media_type`\n46\t  (default `application/json`);\n47\t- `context`: repository identity, branch, `HEAD` SHA, cwd, and a\n48\t  dirty-worktree fingerprint (sha256 of `git status --porcelain`);\n49\t- `conversation`: namespaced id (`claude-code:<session>`) plus a stable\n50\t  `seq` (source line index) providing conversation ordering — the manifest is\n51\t  currently implicit in these fields rather than a separate object;\n52\t- optional `links` (`redacts`, `supersedes`, `annotates`, ...).\n53\t\n54\tConversation turns preserve visible roles, text, tool calls, and tool results\n55\tas supplied by their capture adapter. The source-native payload is retained\n56\tunder `raw` as an opaque, versioned attachment for lossless export.\n57\t\n58\tReasoning policy: record model reasoning when the provider exposes it\n59\t(Claude Code's thinking text is kept, minus opaque signatures). When the\n60\tprovider instead withholds it behind ciphertext (Codex's `reasoning`\n61\tresponse_items carry `encrypted_content`), the ledger still never\n62\tinterprets or attempts to reconstruct it, but does preserve the ciphertext\n63\tlosslessly, opaquely: a `reasoning`-kind event (0.10.0+) whose `content` is\n64\tonly an opacity marker and whose `raw.data` is the full source line, so a\n65\tconsumer that can decrypt it (only the originating provider, by replaying\n66\tthe blob back through its own API) may do so later. `reasoning` events are\n67\thidden from `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` by default\n68\t(`--with-reasoning` reveals them) but included in `cledger export` like\n69\teverything else, and ride the ledger's normal sync — no special carve-out.\n70\tA `reasoning` item's `summary` field, when the provider populates it (real,\n71\talready-decrypted plaintext, distinct from `encrypted_content`), is split\n72\tout into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same `conversation.seq`\n73\trather than swept into the opaque event, so it is captured and redacted\n74\texactly like any other visible content. Capture-tier redaction and\n75\t`cledger scan` both carry a narrow, field-specific exemption\n76\t(`encrypted_content` only, gated on `kind === \"reasoning\"`) so pattern\n77\tmatching never mangles the ciphertext — every other field, including\n78\t`summary`, is scanned normally. The same non-reconstruction rule applies to\n79\tother otherwise-visible content: Codex `agent_message` items (inter-agent\n80\tmessages) convert with their visible text blocks kept and their\n81\t`encrypted_content` blocks dropped from both `content` and `raw` (not yet\n82\tpreserved opaquely the way standalone `reasoning` items are — a deferred\n83\tfollow-up), leaving a bare type marker so the omission is visible.\n84\t\n85\t## Storage\n86\t\n87\tOne git note per **anchor commit** under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`.\n88\tThe note body is JSONL: one canonical-JSON (sorted-keys) event per line,\n89\tlexicographically sorted, unique.\n90\t\n91\tWhy this shape:\n92\t\n93\t1. **Branch-tied by construction.** Events anchor to `HEAD` at capture time,\n94\t   so conversations ride the commit DAG: merging a branch makes its commits —\n95\t   and therefore their conversations — reachable from the target branch with\n96\t   no sync machinery. `cledger log` scopes to the current branch via\n97\t   `git rev-list` reachability.\n98\t2. **GC-safe, nothing outside git.** All content lives inline in the note\n99\t   blob, reachable from the ref. No orphan data branches, no pointers to\n100\t   stores outside the repository.\n101\t3. **Conflict-free concurrency.** Deterministic serialization + sorted unique\n102\t   lines mean git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes merge strategy (configured per-ref\n103\t   at first append) unions concurrent appends cleanly — the same mechanism\n104\t   git-bug and git-appraise rely on.\n105\t4. **Clean working tree.** Notes never appear in `git status`; sharing is\n106\t   the fetch → `git notes merge -s cat_sort_uniq` → push of that single\n107\t   ref, run by the default-on transport hooks or explicitly via `cledger\n108\t   sync` (the E scan gates the push either way — see \"Transport\").\n109\t\n110\tEvents captured before the first commit exists (unborn `HEAD`) queue in\n111\t`.git/conversation-ledger/pending.jsonl` and flush into the first real\n112\tanchor. Everything under `.git/conversation-ledger/` (pending queue, adapter\n113\tcursors, lock) is local, rebuildable state — never the record of truth.\n114\t\n115\t### Squash merges and history rewrites: re-anchoring\n116\t\n117\tA squash merge (or any rewrite) discards the source branch's commits, so\n118\ttheir conversations remain in the ledger (notes are enumerable regardless of\n119\treachability; `cledger log --all` sees them) but would drop out of the target\n120\tbranch's reachability view. Re-anchoring (0.8.0) repairs this with the same\n121\tappend-plus-companion-event shape redaction uses — the union merge would\n122\tresurrect any note line a \"move\" deleted, and the original anchor is honest\n123\tprovenance, so notes are never moved:\n124\t\n125\t- A `re_anchor` event asserts `{superseded: [SHAs], successor: SHA, method,\n126\t  branch?}`. It is anchored to the successor commit, so it rides the\n127\t  surviving branch's DAG and is discoverable exactly when the successor is\n128\t  in view. Its identity is deterministic — sorted SHAs, `system` actor, the\n129\t  successor's committer timestamp as `occurred_at` — so independent\n130\t  detection on two machines dedups to a single event.\n131\t- Read-time reachability resolves mappings to a fixpoint: an anchor counts\n132\t  as reachable when the rev reaches it or a mapping whose successor is\n133\t  reachable names it, transitively (a squash commit itself later squashed\n134\t  still resolves). A mapping whose successor is not in view changes nothing.\n135\t- Detection runs lazily inside every read, right after `absorbIncoming`,\n136\t  gated on a cursor over the remote default branch's tip (origin/HEAD, else\n137\t  the current branch's upstream) so the pass only runs when the target\n138\t  moved, and it never throws. For each local branch no longer reachable\n139\t  from the target: tree equality catches a squash of an unmoved target;\n140\t  `git patch-id --stable` over zero-context diffs (cumulative for squashes,\n141\t  per-commit for rebase/bot rewrites) catches the rest. Only exact matches\n142\t  auto-apply; a fingerprint matching two target commits is reported as\n143\t  ambiguous, never guessed — misattribution is worse than orphaning.\n144\t  Branches with no noted commits are skipped (mappings rescue\n145\t  conversations; they do not catalog merges). `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\":\n146\t  false}}` opts out.\n147\t- `cledger re-anchor` is the explicit path: dry-run by default, `--apply`,\n148\t  and `--onto` for what matching cannot assert (maintainer-edited squashes,\n149\t  deleted branches, ambiguity) — those mappings carry the asserting user as\n150\t  actor, and accept full 40-hex superseded SHAs verbatim since the commit\n151\t  objects may already be GC'd while their notes live on. Since 0.9.0 the\n152\t  inexact cases come with evidence-ranked suggestions (forge merge-commit\n153\t  assertion, message corroboration, per-file overlap — see Open questions)\n154\t  feeding that confirm flow.\n155\t\n156\t## Transport\n157\t\n158\tDefault-on, wired by the first capture in a repo (`ensureTransport`, run on\n159\tevery append as cheap re-checks so a remote added later still gets covered;\n160\tnever throws — it runs inside capture):\n161\t\n162\t- **Push half.** A `pre-push` hook calls `cledger transport-push <remote>`,\n163\t  which pushes the ledger ref gated by the layer-E scan. Policy: a finding\n164\t  holds back *only the ledger* — secrets never leave the machine, but a\n165\t  false positive never blocks shipping code; `{\"transport\": {\"strict\":\n166\t  true}}` escalates to aborting the entire push. Any other failure warns\n167\t  and lets the push proceed. Installation is chain-safe: append to an\n168\t  existing shell hook, back off with a one-time warning when\n169\t  `core.hooksPath` or a non-shell hook owns the file. The hook script\n170\t  embeds the installing cledger's absolute node+cli.js path (PATH fallback\n171\t  in the script), treats \"cledger gone\" as success, and a\n172\t  `CLEDGER_INTERNAL` env guard (set by `sync` around its own push) stops\n173\t  the ledger push from re-triggering the hook.\n174\t- **Fetch half.** A `+refs/notes/conversation-ledger:refs/notes/\n175\t  cledger-incoming` refspec on `origin` makes plain `git fetch`/`git pull`\n176\t  stage the remote's ref; `absorbIncoming` folds the staging ref into the\n177\t  local ref lazily at read time (every `readEvents`) via the same\n178\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union — absorption can only add events, and the local\n179\t  ref is never force-overwritten. `cledger sync`'s fetch phase uses the\n180\t  identical staging path.\n181\t- **Opt-out.** `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`\n182\t  (config), or delete the marked hook block. `cledger sync` stays available\n183\t  regardless.\n184\t\n185\t## Capture adapters\n186\t\n187\t`cledger install` registers per-turn hooks globally: Claude Code\n188\t(`Stop`/`SessionEnd` in `~/.claude/settings.json`) and Codex CLI\n189\t(`[[hooks.Stop]]` in `~/.codex/config.toml`). Hooks receive\n190\t`session_id`/`transcript_path`/`cwd` on stdin, no-op silently outside git\n191\trepositories, never fail the user's session, and re-scan tolerantly —\n192\tidempotent ids make duplicate capture harmless. Per-session cursors under\n193\t`.git/conversation-ledger/cursors/` are a pure optimization.\n194\t\n195\t## Privacy and integrity\n196\t\n197\tVisible tool output can contain secrets. The trust boundary is transport:\n198\tnative transcripts already sit in plaintext on the capturing machine, so\n199\tredaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. But once a\n200\tsecret reaches a note, removal is expensive (the notes ref's own history\n201\tretains prior blobs), so prevention layers run at capture and the last\n202\tcheckpoint runs at sync. Content hashes (event ids) make accidental\n203\tmutation detectable.\n204\t\n205\t### Redaction layers\n206\t\n207\tDefense in depth, ordered by where they run and what they may do:\n208\t\n209\t- **A. Capture-time pattern redaction (default on).** A deliberately\n210\t  conservative, versioned ruleset of unambiguous secret formats — prefixed\n211\t  API tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`,\n212\t  `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private-key blocks, JWTs — applied to every\n213\t  draft inside `appendEvents`, before ids are computed. Matches in both\n214\t  `content` and `raw.data` are replaced with a deterministic placeholder\n215\t  (see below). No entropy heuristics here: a false positive silently\n216\t  rewrites the record, which violates the preservation commitment, so\n217\t  capture-tier rules must be near-zero-false-positive by construction.\n218\t- **B. External scanners as test oracle only.** gitleaks/trufflehog-class\n219\t  rulesets are used in the test suite to prove the capture tier catches\n220\t  what it claims (secret corpus in, zero findings out) — never vendored\n221\t  into the runtime path.\n222\t- **C. Env-value masking (opt-in, default off).** Scrub exact values of\n223\t  local environment variables / `.env` entries from captured content.\n224\t  Highest recall for unstructured secrets, but `.env` is often plain\n225\t  config, and the transform depends on machine state — so it is\n226\t  nondeterministic across rescans (id churn is the documented cost of\n227\t  opting in).\n228\t- **D. User pattern rules (config).** Extra redaction regexes merged into\n229\t  the capture tier from config. Path-based exclusion (\"never record reads\n230\t  of `secrets/**`\") is planned; it requires correlating `tool_use` inputs\n231\t  with `tool_result` events and is not in v1.\n232\t- **E. Sync-time scan (default on, tiered).** Before push, scan only the\n233\t  events the remote does not yet have. The default profile runs\n234\t  medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run for events captured\n235\t  under older rules, keyword-anchored assignments, URL credentials); on a\n236\t  hit the push blocks with an interactive finding report — allowlist the\n237\t  false positive (persisted by fingerprint) or `cledger redact` the real\n238\t  secret while purge is still a local operation. Entropy heuristics live\n239\t  behind an opt-in paranoid tier; `--no-scan`/config disables the gate\n240\t  entirely. Scan-tier rules may be noisy precisely because they only warn\n241\t  a human — they never rewrite anything. Finding reports mask the matched\n242\t  span entirely (surrounding context + fingerprint, zero secret characters),\n243\t  so a captured report cannot re-seed the finding it describes.\n244\t- **F. Known-secret learning (opt-in, default off).** Runs at capture like\n245\t  A/C/D, but is *sourced* from the E flow: a `cledger redact --pattern`\n246\t  remembers the exact values it scrubbed in a local, git-invisible store\n247\t  (`.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`), and capture-time redaction\n248\t  exact-matches them out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id.\n249\t  This closes the capture side of the capture/scan feedback loop — a value E\n250\t  catches but A missed can never be re-captured raw once confirmed. Like C it\n251\t  is exact-value and therefore machine-dependent (id churn is the accepted\n252\t  cost); only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content, no reusable\n253\t  value) and sub-8-char values are dropped to avoid over-matching. It stores\n254\t  plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way and can't drive exact\n255\t  matching) but under `.git/` and written `0600`, exactly as local-and-unshared\n256\t  as the transcripts the values came from — consistent with the transport\n257\t  boundary. Off by default: the store is never read or created unless the flag\n258\t  is set. Note the interaction with the id-preservation rule below: once a\n259\t  value is remembered, re-capturing a source line containing it yields\n260\t  *scrubbed* content and therefore a different id, so it no longer dedups\n261\t  against the redacted original — a second scrubbed copy, not a resurrected\n262\t  secret. Id churn is the accepted cost, identical to C.\n263\t\n264\t### Redaction metadata\n265\t\n266\tA capture-time redaction replaces the matched span with\n267\t`[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]` — fingerprint is a truncated\n268\t`sha256(secret)`, letting identical secrets correlate across events without\n269\tbeing recoverable (brute-forceable only for low-entropy values, which the\n270\tcapture tier does not target). The event also carries a `redactions` array\n271\t(rule id, ruleset version, fingerprint, location path); it sits outside the\n272\tidentity subset, since the rewritten `content` already determines the id.\n273\tDeterminism rule: capture-tier redaction must be a pure function of source\n274\tcontent + versioned ruleset, or rescans duplicate events. Ruleset upgrades\n275\ttherefore change ids on forced rescan — same accepted caveat as identity\n276\tstamping; cursors prevent it in normal operation.\n277\t\n278\tA human redaction of an *existing* event (the E flow) instead rewrites the\n279\tnote line and appends a companion `redaction` event with `links.redacts`,\n280\tthen squashes the local notes ref so the prior blob is unreachable —\n281\thonest history without secret retention (when `knownSecrets` is on, a\n282\t`--pattern` redaction also feeds the F store; see above). Post-push purge\n283\t(force-push + collaborator coordination) is deliberately separate, deferred\n284\ttooling.\n285\t\n286\t## Versioning against harness format changes\n287\t\n288\tThree layers exist today:\n289\t\n290\t1. `schema` on every event (`conversation-ledger/v1`) versions the ledger's\n291\t   own envelope.\n292\t2. `raw.format` (`claude-code-jsonl/1`, `codex-rollout-jsonl/2`) versions\n293\t   each adapter's interpretation of its native format; it must be bumped\n294\t   whenever the mapping changes, allowing later reprocessing to know which\n295\t   parser produced an event. (codex `/2`: `agent_message` payloads convert,\n296\t   encrypted blocks omitted — `/1` dropped those lines entirely.)\n297\t3. The native payload inside `raw.data` retains the harness's own version\n298\t   markers (Claude Code lines carry `version`; Codex `session_meta` carries\n299\t   `cli_version`), so captured content can always be re-normalized under a\n300\t   newer mapping without recapture.\n301\t\n302\tDrift detection and raw preservation: adapters are tolerant parsers, but\n303\teach maintains an explicit known-skipped list (bookkeeping/UI line types)\n304\talongside its convertible set; parsed lines matching neither are\n305\t*unrecognized*. Each such line is both counted\n306\tper type for a capture-time warning (`CaptureResult.unrecognized`) and\n307\tpreserved rather than dropped: the adapter emits an `unrecognized` event\n308\twhose `content` is only a `{unrecognized_type}` label and whose `raw.data`\n309\tholds the full source line, versioned by the adapter's native `raw.format`,\n310\tso a later adapter version can re-normalize (and supersede) it. Because\n311\t`raw` is outside the identity subset, distinctness and idempotency come from\n312\t`conversation.seq` (the source line index) exactly as for interpreted turns,\n313\tand format-version bumps never churn ids. Crucially these events ride the\n314\tnormal `appendEvents` path, so the capture-tier redaction stack walks their\n315\t`raw.data` — an unrecognized line is not a bypass around secret redaction.\n316\t`reasoning` events ride the same path too, with one narrow exception: the\n317\t`encrypted_content` field itself is exempt from pattern-matching (see the\n318\treasoning-policy paragraph above) so redaction can never corrupt the\n319\tciphertext this kind exists to preserve. Codex's `reasoning` response_items\n320\tare a third case distinct from both convertible and unrecognized lines:\n321\trecognized but deliberately opaque, captured as their own `reasoning`-kind\n322\tevent (see the reasoning-policy paragraph above) rather than falling into\n323\teither bucket, and never counted toward the drift warning.\n324\t\n325\tRe-normalization (the supersession half): `cledger renormalize` (library\n326\t`renormalize()`, in `renormalize.ts`) turns a preserved line the current\n327\tadapter can now interpret into the `conversation_turn` it should have been.\n328\tFor each `unrecognized` event it routes by `producer.source` to that adapter's\n329\t`renormalizeUnrecognized`, which re-feeds the stored `raw.data` through the\n330\t*same* `convertLine` the live capture loop uses, with the same identity-\n331\tdetermining inputs recovered from the preserved event: `conversation.seq`,\n332\tthe session id (in `raw.data` for Claude Code, in `producer.session_id` for\n333\tCodex), and — for a Codex line with no timestamp of its own — `occurred_at` as\n334\tthe `baseTime` fallback (which is exactly the `sessionBaseTime` value a live\n335\tcapture would compute). The turn therefore gets the byte-identical id a live\n336\tcapture of the same line would, so a later live capture dedups against it\n337\tinstead of duplicating — the property the whole scheme rests on. When the\n338\tadapter still cannot interpret the line (`convertLine` returns null, e.g. a\n339\ttimestampless Claude line, or a genuinely unknown type) it stays preserved.\n340\t\n341\tThe rewrite is append-only and idempotent: the `unrecognized` event is never\n342\tdeleted but superseded by a `supersession` event carrying\n343\t`links:[{rel:\"supersedes\",target}]` (mirroring how `cledger redact` records a\n344\trewrite via a companion event). Re-runs are no-ops — already-superseded events\n345\tare skipped, and every produced id (turn and supersession) is deterministic,\n346\tso anything that slips through dedups on append. The reconstructed turn's\n347\t`raw.data` is the already-redacted stored copy and rides the normal\n348\t`appendEvents` redaction path, which is idempotent on placeholdered text, so\n349\tnothing is re-exposed and the recomputed id is stable. `reasoning` payloads\n350\tnever reach this path at all — they are captured directly as their own\n351\t`reasoning`-kind event, never as `unrecognized`, and `convertLine` refuses\n352\tthem regardless — so re-normalization never reconstructs provider-withheld\n353\tcontent into a visible `conversation_turn`. What does not exist yet: running this automatically\n354\ton capture when an adapter/version bump is detected — it is a manual command\n355\tfor now (see the format-drift roadmap item).\n356\t\n357\t## Open questions\n358\t\n359\t- Path-based capture exclusion (the path half of layer D): requires\n360\t  correlating tool_use file paths with their tool_result events.\n361\t- Post-push purge tooling (force-push the notes ref + collaborator\n362\t  re-fetch coordination).\n363\t- Transport currently wires `origin` only; fork workflows (per-remote\n364\t  staging refs, refspecs on other remotes) are unhandled — the pre-push\n365\t  hook does push to whichever remote is being pushed, but fetch staging is\n366\t  origin-scoped.\n367\t- Sub-turn citation anchors for downstream consumers (intent-recall).\n368\t- Re-anchoring shipped as default behavior in 0.8.0, and its suggestion\n369\t  tier in 0.9.0 (see \"Squash merges and history rewrites\"): unmatched\n370\t  branches get evidence-ranked candidates — the forge's own merge-commit\n371\t  record for the branch's PR (GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session in\n372\t  `src/forge/`, degrading to offline evidence without it), `(#N)` subject\n373\t  and squash-message corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap — printed\n374\t  with the conversation-carrying commits named and a ready-to-run `--onto`\n375\t  command; confirm-only, and forge lookups never run in the auto read path.\n376\t  Remaining: the accepted cursor gap, where conversations captured onto an\n377\t  already-dead branch only auto-map on the next target move (`cledger\n378\t  re-anchor` covers it manually).\n379\t- Whether to preserve non-git-controlled harness artifacts that die with a\n380\t  worktree (agent memory directories, session state) as `document` events.\n381\t- Whether an explicit `Conversation` manifest object earns its keep once\n382\t  multiple producers exist.\n383\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZG9YAAnaVQs3VbSFh6j5W","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"a61d7e48f18594b62ae378267837151b1827b246","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","seq":35},"id":"ev1-256c7e33c4257188cce009c1f2d8b8c77319e6aa9def3b2cae9bec328ca34b81","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-24T05:01:21.323Z","producer":{"session_id":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.10.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# Technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** implemented in v0.1; format may still evolve until v1.\n4\t\n5\t## Scope\n6\t\n7\tConversation Ledger defines a canonical append-only event format plus a local,\n8\tgit-backed storage protocol. It preserves exact visible content while staying\n9\tout of the working tree. Transport is on by default and rides normal git use:\n10\ta pre-push hook pushes the ledger ref alongside `git push` (scan-gated), and\n11\ta fetch refspec stages the remote's ref for lazy merge at read time (see\n12\t\"Transport\"); `cledger sync` remains the explicit path and the fallback when\n13\thooks are declined.\n14\t\n15\t## Canonical objects\n16\t\n17\tAn `EvidenceEvent` is immutable and has:\n18\t\n19\t- `id`: `ev1-` + sha256 of the event's *identity subset* — the durable,\n20\t  source-determined fields (`kind`, `occurred_at`, `actor.type/id`,\n21\t  `producer.source/session_id`, `conversation`, `media_type`, `content`,\n22\t  `links`). Volatile provenance (`recorded_at`, `context`, `raw`,\n23\t  `producer.tool/version`) is excluded, so re-scanning the same source\n24\t  material always yields the same id: capture is idempotent by construction,\n25\t  and the id doubles as the content hash that makes mutation detectable.\n26\t- `schema` version (`conversation-ledger/v1`);\n27\t- `kind`: `conversation_turn`, `decision`, `document`, `annotation`,\n28\t  `redaction`, or `supersession` — an open string; unknown kinds are stored\n29\t  verbatim so downstream tools can extend without a schema release;\n30\t- `occurred_at` (from the source) and `recorded_at` (at append) timestamps;\n31\t- `actor` (human/agent/system + identity) and `producer` (capture tool,\n32\t  source system, native session id) provenance; adapters stamp human turns\n33\t  with the identity a commit made right now would be authored under,\n34\t  resolved by git itself (`git -c user.useConfigOnly=true var\n35\t  GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT`) in git's own precedence: `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL` env, then\n36\t  `user.email` config (includeIf and all), then `EMAIL` env — never a\n37\t  hostname/OS-username guess, which churns (DHCP renames) and would churn\n38\t  event ids. Explicit config is the fallback when strict resolution\n39\t  refuses over one missing field (email set, no name anywhere). When git\n40\t  would have to guess the email too, turns stay unattributed;\n41\t  `cledger install` warns. Because `actor.id` is part of the event identity subset, sessions\n42\t  captured before this stamping existed will produce new event ids if fully\n43\t  re-scanned — the per-session cursor normally prevents that, but a forced\n44\t  rescan can duplicate pre-identity turns;\n45\t- `content`, stored inline, with an optional `media_type`\n46\t  (default `application/json`);\n47\t- `context`: repository identity, branch, `HEAD` SHA, cwd, and a\n48\t  dirty-worktree fingerprint (sha256 of `git status --porcelain`);\n49\t- `conversation`: namespaced id (`claude-code:<session>`) plus a stable\n50\t  `seq` (source line index) providing conversation ordering — the manifest is\n51\t  currently implicit in these fields rather than a separate object;\n52\t- optional `links` (`redacts`, `supersedes`, `annotates`, ...).\n53\t\n54\tConversation turns preserve visible roles, text, tool calls, and tool results\n55\tas supplied by their capture adapter. The source-native payload is retained\n56\tunder `raw` as an opaque, versioned attachment for lossless export.\n57\t\n58\tReasoning policy: record model reasoning when the provider exposes it\n59\t(Claude Code's thinking text is kept, minus opaque signatures). When the\n60\tprovider instead withholds it behind ciphertext (Codex's `reasoning`\n61\tresponse_items carry `encrypted_content`), the ledger still never\n62\tinterprets or attempts to reconstruct it, but does preserve the ciphertext\n63\tlosslessly, opaquely: a `reasoning`-kind event (0.10.0+) whose `content` is\n64\tonly an opacity marker and whose `raw.data` is the full source line, so a\n65\tconsumer that can decrypt it (only the originating provider, by replaying\n66\tthe blob back through its own API) may do so later. `reasoning` events are\n67\thidden from `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` by default\n68\t(`--with-reasoning` reveals them) but included in `cledger export` like\n69\teverything else, and ride the ledger's normal sync — no special carve-out.\n70\tA `reasoning` item's `summary` field, when the provider populates it (real,\n71\talready-decrypted plaintext, distinct from `encrypted_content`), is split\n72\tout into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same `conversation.seq`\n73\trather than swept into the opaque event, so it is captured and redacted\n74\texactly like any other visible content. Capture-tier redaction and\n75\t`cledger scan` both carry a narrow, field-specific exemption\n76\t(`encrypted_content` only, gated on `kind === \"reasoning\"`) so pattern\n77\tmatching never mangles the ciphertext — every other field, including\n78\t`summary`, is scanned normally. The same non-reconstruction rule applies to\n79\tother otherwise-visible content: Codex `agent_message` items (inter-agent\n80\tmessages) convert with their visible text blocks kept and their\n81\t`encrypted_content` blocks dropped from both `content` and `raw` (not yet\n82\tpreserved opaquely the way standalone `reasoning` items are — a deferred\n83\tfollow-up), leaving a bare type marker so the omission is visible.\n84\t\n85\t## Storage\n86\t\n87\tOne git note per **anchor commit** under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`.\n88\tThe note body is JSONL: one canonical-JSON (sorted-keys) event per line,\n89\tlexicographically sorted, unique.\n90\t\n91\tWhy this shape:\n92\t\n93\t1. **Branch-tied by construction.** Events anchor to `HEAD` at capture time,\n94\t   so conversations ride the commit DAG: merging a branch makes its commits —\n95\t   and therefore their conversations — reachable from the target branch with\n96\t   no sync machinery. `cledger log` scopes to the current branch via\n97\t   `git rev-list` reachability.\n98\t2. **GC-safe, nothing outside git.** All content lives inline in the note\n99\t   blob, reachable from the ref. No orphan data branches, no pointers to\n100\t   stores outside the repository.\n101\t3. **Conflict-free concurrency.** Deterministic serialization + sorted unique\n102\t   lines mean git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes merge strategy (configured per-ref\n103\t   at first append) unions concurrent appends cleanly — the same mechanism\n104\t   git-bug and git-appraise rely on.\n105\t4. **Clean working tree.** Notes never appear in `git status`; sharing is\n106\t   the fetch → `git notes merge -s cat_sort_uniq` → push of that single\n107\t   ref, run by the default-on transport hooks or explicitly via `cledger\n108\t   sync` (the E scan gates the push either way — see \"Transport\").\n109\t\n110\tEvents captured before the first commit exists (unborn `HEAD`) queue in\n111\t`.git/conversation-ledger/pending.jsonl` and flush into the first real\n112\tanchor. Everything under `.git/conversation-ledger/` (pending queue, adapter\n113\tcursors, lock) is local, rebuildable state — never the record of truth.\n114\t\n115\t### Squash merges and history rewrites: re-anchoring\n116\t\n117\tA squash merge (or any rewrite) discards the source branch's commits, so\n118\ttheir conversations remain in the ledger (notes are enumerable regardless of\n119\treachability; `cledger log --all` sees them) but would drop out of the target\n120\tbranch's reachability view. Re-anchoring (0.8.0) repairs this with the same\n121\tappend-plus-companion-event shape redaction uses — the union merge would\n122\tresurrect any note line a \"move\" deleted, and the original anchor is honest\n123\tprovenance, so notes are never moved:\n124\t\n125\t- A `re_anchor` event asserts `{superseded: [SHAs], successor: SHA, method,\n126\t  branch?}`. It is anchored to the successor commit, so it rides the\n127\t  surviving branch's DAG and is discoverable exactly when the successor is\n128\t  in view. Its identity is deterministic — sorted SHAs, `system` actor, the\n129\t  successor's committer timestamp as `occurred_at` — so independent\n130\t  detection on two machines dedups to a single event.\n131\t- Read-time reachability resolves mappings to a fixpoint: an anchor counts\n132\t  as reachable when the rev reaches it or a mapping whose successor is\n133\t  reachable names it, transitively (a squash commit itself later squashed\n134\t  still resolves). A mapping whose successor is not in view changes nothing.\n135\t- Detection runs lazily inside every read, right after `absorbIncoming`,\n136\t  gated on a cursor over the remote default branch's tip (origin/HEAD, else\n137\t  the current branch's upstream) so the pass only runs when the target\n138\t  moved, and it never throws. For each local branch no longer reachable\n139\t  from the target: tree equality catches a squash of an unmoved target;\n140\t  `git patch-id --stable` over zero-context diffs (cumulative for squashes,\n141\t  per-commit for rebase/bot rewrites) catches the rest. Only exact matches\n142\t  auto-apply; a fingerprint matching two target commits is reported as\n143\t  ambiguous, never guessed — misattribution is worse than orphaning.\n144\t  Branches with no noted commits are skipped (mappings rescue\n145\t  conversations; they do not catalog merges). `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\":\n146\t  false}}` opts out.\n147\t- `cledger re-anchor` is the explicit path: dry-run by default, `--apply`,\n148\t  and `--onto` for what matching cannot assert (maintainer-edited squashes,\n149\t  deleted branches, ambiguity) — those mappings carry the asserting user as\n150\t  actor, and accept full 40-hex superseded SHAs verbatim since the commit\n151\t  objects may already be GC'd while their notes live on. Since 0.9.0 the\n152\t  inexact cases come with evidence-ranked suggestions (forge merge-commit\n153\t  assertion, message corroboration, per-file overlap — see Open questions)\n154\t  feeding that confirm flow.\n155\t\n156\t## Transport\n157\t\n158\tDefault-on, wired by the first capture in a repo (`ensureTransport`, run on\n159\tevery append as cheap re-checks so a remote added later still gets covered;\n160\tnever throws — it runs inside capture):\n161\t\n162\t- **Push half.** A `pre-push` hook calls `cledger transport-push <remote>`,\n163\t  which pushes the ledger ref gated by the layer-E scan. Policy: a finding\n164\t  holds back *only the ledger* — secrets never leave the machine, but a\n165\t  false positive never blocks shipping code; `{\"transport\": {\"strict\":\n166\t  true}}` escalates to aborting the entire push. Any other failure warns\n167\t  and lets the push proceed. Installation is chain-safe: append to an\n168\t  existing shell hook, back off with a one-time warning when\n169\t  `core.hooksPath` or a non-shell hook owns the file. The hook script\n170\t  embeds the installing cledger's absolute node+cli.js path (PATH fallback\n171\t  in the script), treats \"cledger gone\" as success, and a\n172\t  `CLEDGER_INTERNAL` env guard (set by `sync` around its own push) stops\n173\t  the ledger push from re-triggering the hook.\n174\t- **Fetch half.** A `+refs/notes/conversation-ledger:refs/notes/\n175\t  cledger-incoming` refspec on `origin` makes plain `git fetch`/`git pull`\n176\t  stage the remote's ref; `absorbIncoming` folds the staging ref into the\n177\t  local ref lazily at read time (every `readEvents`) via the same\n178\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union — absorption can only add events, and the local\n179\t  ref is never force-overwritten. `cledger sync`'s fetch phase uses the\n180\t  identical staging path.\n181\t- **Opt-out.** `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`\n182\t  (config), or delete the marked hook block. `cledger sync` stays available\n183\t  regardless.\n184\t\n185\t## Capture adapters\n186\t\n187\t`cledger install` registers per-turn hooks globally: Claude Code\n188\t(`Stop`/`SessionEnd` in `~/.claude/settings.json`) and Codex CLI\n189\t(`[[hooks.Stop]]` in `~/.codex/config.toml`). Hooks receive\n190\t`session_id`/`transcript_path`/`cwd` on stdin, no-op silently outside git\n191\trepositories, never fail the user's session, and re-scan tolerantly —\n192\tidempotent ids make duplicate capture harmless. Per-session cursors under\n193\t`.git/conversation-ledger/cursors/` are a pure optimization.\n194\t\n195\t## Privacy and integrity\n196\t\n197\tVisible tool output can contain secrets. The trust boundary is transport:\n198\tnative transcripts already sit in plaintext on the capturing machine, so\n199\tredaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. But once a\n200\tsecret reaches a note, removal is expensive (the notes ref's own history\n201\tretains prior blobs), so prevention layers run at capture and the last\n202\tcheckpoint runs at sync. Content hashes (event ids) make accidental\n203\tmutation detectable.\n204\t\n205\t### Redaction layers\n206\t\n207\tDefense in depth, ordered by where they run and what they may do:\n208\t\n209\t- **A. Capture-time pattern redaction (default on).** A deliberately\n210\t  conservative, versioned ruleset of unambiguous secret formats — prefixed\n211\t  API tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`,\n212\t  `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private-key blocks, JWTs — applied to every\n213\t  draft inside `appendEvents`, before ids are computed. Matches in both\n214\t  `content` and `raw.data` are replaced with a deterministic placeholder\n215\t  (see below). No entropy heuristics here: a false positive silently\n216\t  rewrites the record, which violates the preservation commitment, so\n217\t  capture-tier rules must be near-zero-false-positive by construction.\n218\t- **B. External scanners as test oracle only.** gitleaks/trufflehog-class\n219\t  rulesets are used in the test suite to prove the capture tier catches\n220\t  what it claims (secret corpus in, zero findings out) — never vendored\n221\t  into the runtime path.\n222\t- **C. Env-value masking (opt-in, default off).** Scrub exact values of\n223\t  local environment variables / `.env` entries from captured content.\n224\t  Highest recall for unstructured secrets, but `.env` is often plain\n225\t  config, and the transform depends on machine state — so it is\n226\t  nondeterministic across rescans (id churn is the documented cost of\n227\t  opting in).\n228\t- **D. User pattern rules (config).** Extra redaction regexes merged into\n229\t  the capture tier from config. Path-based exclusion (\"never record reads\n230\t  of `secrets/**`\") is planned; it requires correlating `tool_use` inputs\n231\t  with `tool_result` events and is not in v1.\n232\t- **E. Sync-time scan (default on, tiered).** Before push, scan only the\n233\t  events the remote does not yet have. The default profile runs\n234\t  medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run for events captured\n235\t  under older rules, keyword-anchored assignments, URL credentials); on a\n236\t  hit the push blocks with an interactive finding report — allowlist the\n237\t  false positive (persisted by fingerprint) or `cledger redact` the real\n238\t  secret while purge is still a local operation. Entropy heuristics live\n239\t  behind an opt-in paranoid tier; `--no-scan`/config disables the gate\n240\t  entirely. Scan-tier rules may be noisy precisely because they only warn\n241\t  a human — they never rewrite anything. Finding reports mask the matched\n242\t  span entirely (surrounding context + fingerprint, zero secret characters),\n243\t  so a captured report cannot re-seed the finding it describes.\n244\t- **F. Known-secret learning (opt-in, default off).** Runs at capture like\n245\t  A/C/D, but is *sourced* from the E flow: a `cledger redact --pattern`\n246\t  remembers the exact values it scrubbed in a local, git-invisible store\n247\t  (`.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`), and capture-time redaction\n248\t  exact-matches them out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id.\n249\t  This closes the capture side of the capture/scan feedback loop — a value E\n250\t  catches but A missed can never be re-captured raw once confirmed. Like C it\n251\t  is exact-value and therefore machine-dependent (id churn is the accepted\n252\t  cost); only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content, no reusable\n253\t  value) and sub-8-char values are dropped to avoid over-matching. It stores\n254\t  plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way and can't drive exact\n255\t  matching) but under `.git/` and written `0600`, exactly as local-and-unshared\n256\t  as the transcripts the values came from — consistent with the transport\n257\t  boundary. Off by default: the store is never read or created unless the flag\n258\t  is set. Note the interaction with the id-preservation rule below: once a\n259\t  value is remembered, re-capturing a source line containing it yields\n260\t  *scrubbed* content and therefore a different id, so it no longer dedups\n261\t  against the redacted original — a second scrubbed copy, not a resurrected\n262\t  secret. Id churn is the accepted cost, identical to C.\n263\t\n264\t### Redaction metadata\n265\t\n266\tA capture-time redaction replaces the matched span with\n267\t`[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]` — fingerprint is a truncated\n268\t`sha256(secret)`, letting identical secrets correlate across events without\n269\tbeing recoverable (brute-forceable only for low-entropy values, which the\n270\tcapture tier does not target). The event also carries a `redactions` array\n271\t(rule id, ruleset version, fingerprint, location path); it sits outside the\n272\tidentity subset, since the rewritten `content` already determines the id.\n273\tDeterminism rule: capture-tier redaction must be a pure function of source\n274\tcontent + versioned ruleset, or rescans duplicate events. Ruleset upgrades\n275\ttherefore change ids on forced rescan — same accepted caveat as identity\n276\tstamping; cursors prevent it in normal operation.\n277\t\n278\tA human redaction of an *existing* event (the E flow) instead rewrites the\n279\tnote line and appends a companion `redaction` event with `links.redacts`,\n280\tthen squashes the local notes ref so the prior blob is unreachable —\n281\thonest history without secret retention (when `knownSecrets` is on, a\n282\t`--pattern` redaction also feeds the F store; see above). Post-push purge\n283\t(force-push + collaborator coordination) is deliberately separate, deferred\n284\ttooling.\n285\t\n286\t## Versioning against harness format changes\n287\t\n288\tThree layers exist today:\n289\t\n290\t1. `schema` on every event (`conversation-ledger/v1`) versions the ledger's\n291\t   own envelope.\n292\t2. `raw.format` (`claude-code-jsonl/1`, `codex-rollout-jsonl/2`) versions\n293\t   each adapter's interpretation of its native format; it must be bumped\n294\t   whenever the mapping changes, allowing later reprocessing to know which\n295\t   parser produced an event. (codex `/2`: `agent_message` payloads convert,\n296\t   encrypted blocks omitted — `/1` dropped those lines entirely.)\n297\t3. The native payload inside `raw.data` retains the harness's own version\n298\t   markers (Claude Code lines carry `version`; Codex `session_meta` carries\n299\t   `cli_version`), so captured content can always be re-normalized under a\n300\t   newer mapping without recapture.\n301\t\n302\tDrift detection and raw preservation: adapters are tolerant parsers, but\n303\teach maintains an explicit known-skipped list (bookkeeping/UI line types)\n304\talongside its convertible set; parsed lines matching neither are\n305\t*unrecognized*. Each such line is both counted\n306\tper type for a capture-time warning (`CaptureResult.unrecognized`) and\n307\tpreserved rather than dropped: the adapter emits an `unrecognized` event\n308\twhose `content` is only a `{unrecognized_type}` label and whose `raw.data`\n309\tholds the full source line, versioned by the adapter's native `raw.format`,\n310\tso a later adapter version can re-normalize (and supersede) it. Because\n311\t`raw` is outside the identity subset, distinctness and idempotency come from\n312\t`conversation.seq` (the source line index) exactly as for interpreted turns,\n313\tand format-version bumps never churn ids. Crucially these events ride the\n314\tnormal `appendEvents` path, so the capture-tier redaction stack walks their\n315\t`raw.data` — an unrecognized line is not a bypass around secret redaction.\n316\t`reasoning` events ride the same path too, with one narrow exception: the\n317\t`encrypted_content` field itself is exempt from pattern-matching (see the\n318\treasoning-policy paragraph above) so redaction can never corrupt the\n319\tciphertext this kind exists to preserve. Codex's `reasoning` response_items\n320\tare a third case distinct from both convertible and unrecognized lines:\n321\trecognized but deliberately opaque, captured as their own `reasoning`-kind\n322\tevent (see the reasoning-policy paragraph above) rather than falling into\n323\teither bucket, and never counted toward the drift warning.\n324\t\n325\tRe-normalization (the supersession half): `cledger renormalize` (library\n326\t`renormalize()`, in `renormalize.ts`) turns a preserved line the current\n327\tadapter can now interpret into the `conversation_turn` it should have been.\n328\tFor each `unrecognized` event it routes by `producer.source` to that adapter's\n329\t`renormalizeUnrecognized`, which re-feeds the stored `raw.data` through the\n330\t*same* `convertLine` the live capture loop uses, with the same identity-\n331\tdetermining inputs recovered from the preserved event: `conversation.seq`,\n332\tthe session id (in `raw.data` for Claude Code, in `producer.session_id` for\n333\tCodex), and — for a Codex line with no timestamp of its own — `occurred_at` as\n334\tthe `baseTime` fallback (which is exactly the `sessionBaseTime` value a live\n335\tcapture would compute). The turn therefore gets the byte-identical id a live\n336\tcapture of the same line would, so a later live capture dedups against it\n337\tinstead of duplicating — the property the whole scheme rests on. When the\n338\tadapter still cannot interpret the line (`convertLine` returns null, e.g. a\n339\ttimestampless Claude line, or a genuinely unknown type) it stays preserved.\n340\t\n341\tThe rewrite is append-only and idempotent: the `unrecognized` event is never\n342\tdeleted but superseded by a `supersession` event carrying\n343\t`links:[{rel:\"supersedes\",target}]` (mirroring how `cledger redact` records a\n344\trewrite via a companion event). Re-runs are no-ops — already-superseded events\n345\tare skipped, and every produced id (turn and supersession) is deterministic,\n346\tso anything that slips through dedups on append. The reconstructed turn's\n347\t`raw.data` is the already-redacted stored copy and rides the normal\n348\t`appendEvents` redaction path, which is idempotent on placeholdered text, so\n349\tnothing is re-exposed and the recomputed id is stable. `reasoning` payloads\n350\tnever reach this path at all — they are captured directly as their own\n351\t`reasoning`-kind event, never as `unrecognized`, and `convertLine` refuses\n352\tthem regardless — so re-normalization never reconstructs provider-withheld\n353\tcontent into a visible `conversation_turn`. What does not exist yet: running this automatically\n354\ton capture when an adapter/version bump is detected — it is a manual command\n355\tfor now (see the format-drift roadmap item).\n356\t\n357\t## Open questions\n358\t\n359\t- Path-based capture exclusion (the path half of layer D): requires\n360\t  correlating tool_use file paths with their tool_result events.\n361\t- Post-push purge tooling (force-push the notes ref + collaborator\n362\t  re-fetch coordination).\n363\t- Transport currently wires `origin` only; fork workflows (per-remote\n364\t  staging refs, refspecs on other remotes) are unhandled — the pre-push\n365\t  hook does push to whichever remote is being pushed, but fetch staging is\n366\t  origin-scoped.\n367\t- Sub-turn citation anchors for downstream consumers (intent-recall).\n368\t- Re-anchoring shipped as default behavior in 0.8.0, and its suggestion\n369\t  tier in 0.9.0 (see \"Squash merges and history rewrites\"): unmatched\n370\t  branches get evidence-ranked candidates — the forge's own merge-commit\n371\t  record for the branch's PR (GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session in\n372\t  `src/forge/`, degrading to offline evidence without it), `(#N)` subject\n373\t  and squash-message corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap — printed\n374\t  with the conversation-carrying commits named and a ready-to-run `--onto`\n375\t  command; confirm-only, and forge lookups never run in the auto read path.\n376\t  Remaining: the accepted cursor gap, where conversations captured onto an\n377\t  already-dead branch only auto-map on the next target move (`cledger\n378\t  re-anchor` covers it manually).\n379\t- Whether to preserve non-git-controlled harness artifacts that die with a\n380\t  worktree (agent memory directories, session state) as `document` events.\n381\t- Whether an explicit `Conversation` manifest object earns its keep once\n382\t  multiple producers exist.\n383\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZG9YAAnaVQs3VbSFh6j5W","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"2aee2371-2546-4834-a54b-e786f2b54819","promptId":"c1b4f319-a8fa-4bf4-a300-2234b56ef2c1","sessionId":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","session_id":"43ec7768-b4ea-485b-b6e2-ed60c3ad4a01","slug":"vectorized-hopping-possum","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2aee2371-2546-4834-a54b-e786f2b54819","timestamp":"2026-07-24T05:01:21.323Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# Technical design\n\n**Status:** implemented in v0.1; format may still evolve until v1.\n\n## Scope\n\nConversation Ledger defines a canonical append-only event format plus a local,\ngit-backed storage protocol. It preserves exact visible content while staying\nout of the working tree. Transport is on by default and rides normal git use:\na pre-push hook pushes the ledger ref alongside `git push` (scan-gated), and\na fetch refspec stages the remote's ref for lazy merge at read time (see\n\"Transport\"); `cledger sync` remains the explicit path and the fallback when\nhooks are declined.\n\n## Canonical objects\n\nAn `EvidenceEvent` is immutable and has:\n\n- `id`: `ev1-` + sha256 of the event's *identity subset* — the durable,\n  source-determined fields (`kind`, `occurred_at`, `actor.type/id`,\n  `producer.source/session_id`, `conversation`, `media_type`, `content`,\n  `links`). Volatile provenance (`recorded_at`, `context`, `raw`,\n  `producer.tool/version`) is excluded, so re-scanning the same source\n  material always yields the same id: capture is idempotent by construction,\n  and the id doubles as the content hash that makes mutation detectable.\n- `schema` version (`conversation-ledger/v1`);\n- `kind`: `conversation_turn`, `decision`, `document`, `annotation`,\n  `redaction`, or `supersession` — an open string; unknown kinds are stored\n  verbatim so downstream tools can extend without a schema release;\n- `occurred_at` (from the source) and `recorded_at` (at append) timestamps;\n- `actor` (human/agent/system + identity) and `producer` (capture tool,\n  source system, native session id) provenance; adapters stamp human turns\n  with the identity a commit made right now would be authored under,\n  resolved by git itself (`git -c user.useConfigOnly=true var\n  GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT`) in git's own precedence: `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL` env, then\n  `user.email` config (includeIf and all), then `EMAIL` env — never a\n  hostname/OS-username guess, which churns (DHCP renames) and would churn\n  event ids. Explicit config is the fallback when strict resolution\n  refuses over one missing field (email set, no name anywhere). When git\n  would have to guess the email too, turns stay unattributed;\n  `cledger install` warns. Because `actor.id` is part of the event identity subset, sessions\n  captured before this stamping existed will produce new event ids if fully\n  re-scanned — the per-session cursor normally prevents that, but a forced\n  rescan can duplicate pre-identity turns;\n- `content`, stored inline, with an optional `media_type`\n  (default `application/json`);\n- `context`: repository identity, branch, `HEAD` SHA, cwd, and a\n  dirty-worktree fingerprint (sha256 of `git status --porcelain`);\n- `conversation`: namespaced id (`claude-code:<session>`) plus a stable\n  `seq` (source line index) providing conversation ordering — the manifest is\n  currently implicit in these fields rather than a separate object;\n- optional `links` (`redacts`, `supersedes`, `annotates`, ...).\n\nConversation turns preserve visible roles, text, tool calls, and tool results\nas supplied by their capture adapter. The source-native payload is retained\nunder `raw` as an opaque, versioned attachment for lossless export.\n\nReasoning policy: record model reasoning when the provider exposes it\n(Claude Code's thinking text is kept, minus opaque signatures). When the\nprovider instead withholds it behind ciphertext (Codex's `reasoning`\nresponse_items carry `encrypted_content`), the ledger still never\ninterprets or attempts to reconstruct it, but does preserve the ciphertext\nlosslessly, opaquely: a `reasoning`-kind event (0.10.0+) whose `content` is\nonly an opacity marker and whose `raw.data` is the full source line, so a\nconsumer that can decrypt it (only the originating provider, by replaying\nthe blob back through its own API) may do so later. `reasoning` events are\nhidden from `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` by default\n(`--with-reasoning` reveals them) but included in `cledger export` like\neverything else, and ride the ledger's normal sync — no special carve-out.\nA `reasoning` item's `summary` field, when the provider populates it (real,\nalready-decrypted plaintext, distinct from `encrypted_content`), is split\nout into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same `conversation.seq`\nrather than swept into the opaque event, so it is captured and redacted\nexactly like any other visible content. Capture-tier redaction and\n`cledger scan` both carry a narrow, field-specific exemption\n(`encrypted_content` only, gated on `kind === \"reasoning\"`) so pattern\nmatching never mangles the ciphertext — every other field, including\n`summary`, is scanned normally. The same non-reconstruction rule applies to\nother otherwise-visible content: Codex `agent_message` items (inter-agent\nmessages) convert with their visible text blocks kept and their\n`encrypted_content` blocks dropped from both `content` and `raw` (not yet\npreserved opaquely the way standalone `reasoning` items are — a deferred\nfollow-up), leaving a bare type marker so the omission is visible.\n\n## Storage\n\nOne git note per **anchor commit** under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`.\nThe note body is JSONL: one canonical-JSON (sorted-keys) event per line,\nlexicographically sorted, unique.\n\nWhy this shape:\n\n1. **Branch-tied by construction.** Events anchor to `HEAD` at capture time,\n   so conversations ride the commit DAG: merging a branch makes its commits —\n   and therefore their conversations — reachable from the target branch with\n   no sync machinery. `cledger log` scopes to the current branch via\n   `git rev-list` reachability.\n2. **GC-safe, nothing outside git.** All content lives inline in the note\n   blob, reachable from the ref. No orphan data branches, no pointers to\n   stores outside the repository.\n3. **Conflict-free concurrency.** Deterministic serialization + sorted unique\n   lines mean git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes merge strategy (configured per-ref\n   at first append) unions concurrent appends cleanly — the same mechanism\n   git-bug and git-appraise rely on.\n4. **Clean working tree.** Notes never appear in `git status`; sharing is\n   the fetch → `git notes merge -s cat_sort_uniq` → push of that single\n   ref, run by the default-on transport hooks or explicitly via `cledger\n   sync` (the E scan gates the push either way — see \"Transport\").\n\nEvents captured before the first commit exists (unborn `HEAD`) queue in\n`.git/conversation-ledger/pending.jsonl` and flush into the first real\nanchor. Everything under `.git/conversation-ledger/` (pending queue, adapter\ncursors, lock) is local, rebuildable state — never the record of truth.\n\n### Squash merges and history rewrites: re-anchoring\n\nA squash merge (or any rewrite) discards the source branch's commits, so\ntheir conversations remain in the ledger (notes are enumerable regardless of\nreachability; `cledger log --all` sees them) but would drop out of the target\nbranch's reachability view. Re-anchoring (0.8.0) repairs this with the same\nappend-plus-companion-event shape redaction uses — the union merge would\nresurrect any note line a \"move\" deleted, and the original anchor is honest\nprovenance, so notes are never moved:\n\n- A `re_anchor` event asserts `{superseded: [SHAs], successor: SHA, method,\n  branch?}`. It is anchored to the successor commit, so it rides the\n  surviving branch's DAG and is discoverable exactly when the successor is\n  in view. Its identity is deterministic — sorted SHAs, `system` actor, the\n  successor's committer timestamp as `occurred_at` — so independent\n  detection on two machines dedups to a single event.\n- Read-time reachability resolves mappings to a fixpoint: an anchor counts\n  as reachable when the rev reaches it or a mapping whose successor is\n  reachable names it, transitively (a squash commit itself later squashed\n  still resolves). A mapping whose successor is not in view changes nothing.\n- Detection runs lazily inside every read, right after `absorbIncoming`,\n  gated on a cursor over the remote default branch's tip (origin/HEAD, else\n  the current branch's upstream) so the pass only runs when the target\n  moved, and it never throws. For each local branch no longer reachable\n  from the target: tree equality catches a squash of an unmoved target;\n  `git patch-id --stable` over zero-context diffs (cumulative for squashes,\n  per-commit for rebase/bot rewrites) catches the rest. Only exact matches\n  auto-apply; a fingerprint matching two target commits is reported as\n  ambiguous, never guessed — misattribution is worse than orphaning.\n  Branches with no noted commits are skipped (mappings rescue\n  conversations; they do not catalog merges). `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\":\n  false}}` opts out.\n- `cledger re-anchor` is the explicit path: dry-run by default, `--apply`,\n  and `--onto` for what matching cannot assert (maintainer-edited squashes,\n  deleted branches, ambiguity) — those mappings carry the asserting user as\n  actor, and accept full 40-hex superseded SHAs verbatim since the commit\n  objects may already be GC'd while their notes live on. Since 0.9.0 the\n  inexact cases come with evidence-ranked suggestions (forge merge-commit\n  assertion, message corroboration, per-file overlap — see Open questions)\n  feeding that confirm flow.\n\n## Transport\n\nDefault-on, wired by the first capture in a repo (`ensureTransport`, run on\nevery append as cheap re-checks so a remote added later still gets covered;\nnever throws — it runs inside capture):\n\n- **Push half.** A `pre-push` hook calls `cledger transport-push <remote>`,\n  which pushes the ledger ref gated by the layer-E scan. Policy: a finding\n  holds back *only the ledger* — secrets never leave the machine, but a\n  false positive never blocks shipping code; `{\"transport\": {\"strict\":\n  true}}` escalates to aborting the entire push. Any other failure warns\n  and lets the push proceed. Installation is chain-safe: append to an\n  existing shell hook, back off with a one-time warning when\n  `core.hooksPath` or a non-shell hook owns the file. The hook script\n  embeds the installing cledger's absolute node+cli.js path (PATH fallback\n  in the script), treats \"cledger gone\" as success, and a\n  `CLEDGER_INTERNAL` env guard (set by `sync` around its own push) stops\n  the ledger push from re-triggering the hook.\n- **Fetch half.** A `+refs/notes/conversation-ledger:refs/notes/\n  cledger-incoming` refspec on `origin` makes plain `git fetch`/`git pull`\n  stage the remote's ref; `absorbIncoming` folds the staging ref into the\n  local ref lazily at read time (every `readEvents`) via the same\n  `cat_sort_uniq` union — absorption can only add events, and the local\n  ref is never force-overwritten. `cledger sync`'s fetch phase uses the\n  identical staging path.\n- **Opt-out.** `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`\n  (config), or delete the marked hook block. `cledger sync` stays available\n  regardless.\n\n## Capture adapters\n\n`cledger install` registers per-turn hooks globally: Claude Code\n(`Stop`/`SessionEnd` in `~/.claude/settings.json`) and Codex CLI\n(`[[hooks.Stop]]` in `~/.codex/config.toml`). Hooks receive\n`session_id`/`transcript_path`/`cwd` on stdin, no-op silently outside git\nrepositories, never fail the user's session, and re-scan tolerantly —\nidempotent ids make duplicate capture harmless. Per-session cursors under\n`.git/conversation-ledger/cursors/` are a pure optimization.\n\n## Privacy and integrity\n\nVisible tool output can contain secrets. The trust boundary is transport:\nnative transcripts already sit in plaintext on the capturing machine, so\nredaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. But once a\nsecret reaches a note, removal is expensive (the notes ref's own history\nretains prior blobs), so prevention layers run at capture and the last\ncheckpoint runs at sync. Content hashes (event ids) make accidental\nmutation detectable.\n\n### Redaction layers\n\nDefense in depth, ordered by where they run and what they may do:\n\n- **A. Capture-time pattern redaction (default on).** A deliberately\n  conservative, versioned ruleset of unambiguous secret formats — prefixed\n  API tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`,\n  `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private-key blocks, JWTs — applied to every\n  draft inside `appendEvents`, before ids are computed. Matches in both\n  `content` and `raw.data` are replaced with a deterministic placeholder\n  (see below). No entropy heuristics here: a false positive silently\n  rewrites the record, which violates the preservation commitment, so\n  capture-tier rules must be near-zero-false-positive by construction.\n- **B. External scanners as test oracle only.** gitleaks/trufflehog-class\n  rulesets are used in the test suite to prove the capture tier catches\n  what it claims (secret corpus in, zero findings out) — never vendored\n  into the runtime path.\n- **C. Env-value masking (opt-in, default off).** Scrub exact values of\n  local environment variables / `.env` entries from captured content.\n  Highest recall for unstructured secrets, but `.env` is often plain\n  config, and the transform depends on machine state — so it is\n  nondeterministic across rescans (id churn is the documented cost of\n  opting in).\n- **D. User pattern rules (config).** Extra redaction regexes merged into\n  the capture tier from config. Path-based exclusion (\"never record reads\n  of `secrets/**`\") is planned; it requires correlating `tool_use` inputs\n  with `tool_result` events and is not in v1.\n- **E. Sync-time scan (default on, tiered).** Before push, scan only the\n  events the remote does not yet have. The default profile runs\n  medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run for events captured\n  under older rules, keyword-anchored assignments, URL credentials); on a\n  hit the push blocks with an interactive finding report — allowlist the\n  false positive (persisted by fingerprint) or `cledger redact` the real\n  secret while purge is still a local operation. Entropy heuristics live\n  behind an opt-in paranoid tier; `--no-scan`/config disables the gate\n  entirely. Scan-tier rules may be noisy precisely because they only warn\n  a human — they never rewrite anything. Finding reports mask the matched\n  span entirely (surrounding context + fingerprint, zero secret characters),\n  so a captured report cannot re-seed the finding it describes.\n- **F. Known-secret learning (opt-in, default off).** Runs at capture like\n  A/C/D, but is *sourced* from the E flow: a `cledger redact --pattern`\n  remembers the exact values it scrubbed in a local, git-invisible store\n  (`.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`), and capture-time redaction\n  exact-matches them out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id.\n  This closes the capture side of the capture/scan feedback loop — a value E\n  catches but A missed can never be re-captured raw once confirmed. Like C it\n  is exact-value and therefore machine-dependent (id churn is the accepted\n  cost); only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content, no reusable\n  value) and sub-8-char values are dropped to avoid over-matching. It stores\n  plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way and can't drive exact\n  matching) but under `.git/` and written `0600`, exactly as local-and-unshared\n  as the transcripts the values came from — consistent with the transport\n  boundary. Off by default: the store is never read or created unless the flag\n  is set. Note the interaction with the id-preservation rule below: once a\n  value is remembered, re-capturing a source line containing it yields\n  *scrubbed* content and therefore a different id, so it no longer dedups\n  against the redacted original — a second scrubbed copy, not a resurrected\n  secret. Id churn is the accepted cost, identical to C.\n\n### Redaction metadata\n\nA capture-time redaction replaces the matched span with\n`[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]` — fingerprint is a truncated\n`sha256(secret)`, letting identical secrets correlate across events without\nbeing recoverable (brute-forceable only for low-entropy values, which the\ncapture tier does not target). The event also carries a `redactions` array\n(rule id, ruleset version, fingerprint, location path); it sits outside the\nidentity subset, since the rewritten `content` already determines the id.\nDeterminism rule: capture-tier redaction must be a pure function of source\ncontent + versioned ruleset, or rescans duplicate events. Ruleset upgrades\ntherefore change ids on forced rescan — same accepted caveat as identity\nstamping; cursors prevent it in normal operation.\n\nA human redaction of an *existing* event (the E flow) instead rewrites the\nnote line and appends a companion `redaction` event with `links.redacts`,\nthen squashes the local notes ref so the prior blob is unreachable —\nhonest history without secret retention (when `knownSecrets` is on, a\n`--pattern` redaction also feeds the F store; see above). Post-push purge\n(force-push + collaborator coordination) is deliberately separate, deferred\ntooling.\n\n## Versioning against harness format changes\n\nThree layers exist today:\n\n1. `schema` on every event (`conversation-ledger/v1`) versions the ledger's\n   own envelope.\n2. `raw.format` (`claude-code-jsonl/1`, `codex-rollout-jsonl/2`) versions\n   each adapter's interpretation of its native format; it must be bumped\n   whenever the mapping changes, allowing later reprocessing to know which\n   parser produced an event. (codex `/2`: `agent_message` payloads convert,\n   encrypted blocks omitted — `/1` dropped those lines entirely.)\n3. The native payload inside `raw.data` retains the harness's own version\n   markers (Claude Code lines carry `version`; Codex `session_meta` carries\n   `cli_version`), so captured content can always be re-normalized under a\n   newer mapping without recapture.\n\nDrift detection and raw preservation: adapters are tolerant parsers, but\neach maintains an explicit known-skipped list (bookkeeping/UI line types)\nalongside its convertible set; parsed lines matching neither are\n*unrecognized*. Each such line is both counted\nper type for a capture-time warning (`CaptureResult.unrecognized`) and\npreserved rather than dropped: the adapter emits an `unrecognized` event\nwhose `content` is only a `{unrecognized_type}` label and whose `raw.data`\nholds the full source line, versioned by the adapter's native `raw.format`,\nso a later adapter version can re-normalize (and supersede) it. Because\n`raw` is outside the identity subset, distinctness and idempotency come from\n`conversation.seq` (the source line index) exactly as for interpreted turns,\nand format-version bumps never churn ids. Crucially these events ride the\nnormal `appendEvents` path, so the capture-tier redaction stack walks their\n`raw.data` — an unrecognized line is not a bypass around secret redaction.\n`reasoning` events ride the same path too, with one narrow exception: the\n`encrypted_content` field itself is exempt from pattern-matching (see the\nreasoning-policy paragraph above) so redaction can never corrupt the\nciphertext this kind exists to preserve. Codex's `reasoning` response_items\nare a third case distinct from both convertible and unrecognized lines:\nrecognized but deliberately opaque, captured as their own `reasoning`-kind\nevent (see the reasoning-policy paragraph above) rather than falling into\neither bucket, and never counted toward the drift warning.\n\nRe-normalization (the supersession half): `cledger renormalize` (library\n`renormalize()`, in `renormalize.ts`) turns a preserved line the current\nadapter can now interpret into the `conversation_turn` it should have been.\nFor each `unrecognized` event it routes by `producer.source` to that adapter's\n`renormalizeUnrecognized`, which re-feeds the stored `raw.data` through the\n*same* `convertLine` the live capture loop uses, with the same identity-\ndetermining inputs recovered from the preserved event: `conversation.seq`,\nthe session id (in `raw.data` for Claude Code, in `producer.session_id` for\nCodex), and — for a Codex line with no timestamp of its own — `occurred_at` as\nthe `baseTime` fallback (which is exactly the `sessionBaseTime` value a live\ncapture would compute). The turn therefore gets the byte-identical id a live\ncapture of the same line would, so a later live capture dedups against it\ninstead of duplicating — the property the whole scheme rests on. When the\nadapter still cannot interpret the line (`convertLine` returns null, e.g. a\ntimestampless Claude line, or a genuinely unknown type) it stays preserved.\n\nThe rewrite is append-only and idempotent: the `unrecognized` event is never\ndeleted but superseded by a `supersession` event carrying\n`links:[{rel:\"supersedes\",target}]` (mirroring how `cledger redact` records a\nrewrite via a companion event). Re-runs are no-ops — already-superseded events\nare skipped, and every produced id (turn and supersession) is deterministic,\nso anything that slips through dedups on append. The reconstructed turn's\n`raw.data` is the already-redacted stored copy and rides the normal\n`appendEvents` redaction path, which is idempotent on placeholdered text, so\nnothing is re-exposed and the recomputed id is stable. `reasoning` payloads\nnever reach this path at all — they are captured directly as their own\n`reasoning`-kind event, never as `unrecognized`, and `convertLine` refuses\nthem regardless — so re-normalization never reconstructs provider-withheld\ncontent into a visible `conversation_turn`. What does not exist yet: running this automatically\non capture when an adapter/version bump is detected — it is a manual command\nfor now (see the format-drift roadmap item).\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Path-based capture exclusion (the path half of layer D): requires\n  correlating tool_use file paths with their tool_result events.\n- Post-push purge tooling (force-push the notes ref + collaborator\n  re-fetch coordination).\n- Transport currently wires `origin` only; fork workflows (per-remote\n  staging refs, refspecs on other remotes) are unhandled — the pre-push\n  hook does push to whichever remote is being pushed, but fetch staging is\n  origin-scoped.\n- Sub-turn citation anchors for downstream consumers (intent-recall).\n- Re-anchoring shipped as default behavior in 0.8.0, and its suggestion\n  tier in 0.9.0 (see \"Squash merges and history rewrites\"): unmatched\n  branches get evidence-ranked candidates — the forge's own merge-commit\n  record for the branch's PR (GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session in\n  `src/forge/`, degrading to offline evidence without it), `(#N)` subject\n  and squash-message corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap — printed\n  with the conversation-carrying commits named and a ready-to-run `--onto`\n  command; confirm-only, and forge lookups never run in the auto read path.\n  Remaining: the accepted cursor gap, where conversations captured onto an\n  already-dead branch only auto-map on the next target move (`cledger\n  re-anchor` covers it manually).\n- Whether to preserve non-git-controlled harness artifacts that die with a\n  worktree (agent memory directories, session state) as `document` events.\n- Whether an explicit `Conversation` manifest object earns its keep once\n  multiple producers exist.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md","numLines":383,"startLine":1,"totalLines":383},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"ec77cc85-4764-45a3-a1c7-270f6e50eb24","version":"2.1.218"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-07-24T05:03:11.380Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t// Probe: does codex resume accept a REAL encrypted reasoning item replayed\n2\t// inside a fabricated rollout (same account, different session)? This is the\n3\t// load-bearing unknown for recording encrypted_content in cledger: if the\n4\t// API rejects cross-session replay, the capture-policy debate is moot.\n5\timport { spawnSync } from \"node:child_process\";\n6\timport { mkdtemp, readFile, writeFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n7\timport { tmpdir } from \"node:os\";\n8\timport { join } from \"node:path\";\n9\timport process from \"node:process\";\n10\timport { appendEvents, findRepo, git } from \"conversation-ledger\";\n11\timport { listConversations, targetFor } from \"../dist/index.js\";\n12\t\n13\t// Donor: any real rollout containing a `reasoning` response_item with\n14\t// encrypted_content (find one: grep -rl encrypted_content ~/.codex/sessions).\n15\t// Verified 2026-07-21: same-account replay accepted across sessions and\n16\t// across CLI versions (0.144-created blob, resumed under 0.145). Still\n17\t// unverified: cross-account replay (needs a second OpenAI account).\n18\tconst DONOR = process.argv[2];\n19\tif (!DONOR) throw new Error(\"usage: probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs <donor-rollout.jsonl>\");\n20\t\n21\t// 1. Extract a real reasoning line (envelope + payload verbatim) from the donor.\n22\tconst donorLines = (await readFile(DONOR, \"utf8\")).split(\"\\n\").filter(Boolean).map(JSON.parse);\n23\tconst reasoningLine = donorLines.find(\n24\t  (l) => l.type === \"response_item\" && l.payload?.type === \"reasoning\" && l.payload?.encrypted_content,\n25\t);\n26\tif (!reasoningLine) throw new Error(\"donor has no encrypted reasoning response_item\");\n27\tconsole.log(\n28\t  `donor reasoning item: id=${reasoningLine.payload.id ?? \"(none)\"} blob=${reasoningLine.payload.encrypted_content.length} chars`,\n29\t);\n30\t\n31\t// 2. Seed a marker conversation and fabricate a codex session from it.\n32\tconst MARKER = `TB-ENCPROBE-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)}`;\n33\tconst dir = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), \"turnbridge-encprobe-\"));\n34\tawait git([\"init\", \"-q\", \"-b\", \"main\"], { cwd: dir });\n35\tawait git([\"config\", \"user.email\", \"smoke@turnbridge.test\"], { cwd: dir });\n36\tawait git([\"config\", \"user.name\", \"Turnbridge Smoke\"], { cwd: dir });\n37\tawait git([\"config\", \"commit.gpgsign\", \"false\"], { cwd: dir });\n38\tawait writeFile(join(dir, \"README.md\"), \"probe\\n\");\n39\tawait git([\"add\", \".\"], { cwd: dir });\n40\tawait git([\"commit\", \"-q\", \"-m\", \"init\"], { cwd: dir });\n41\tconst repo = await findRepo(dir);\n42\t\n43\tconst conversationId = `claude-code:${crypto.randomUUID()}`;\n44\tconst turn = (role, text, seq) => ({\n45\t  kind: \"conversation_turn\",\n46\t  occurred_at: `2026-01-01T00:00:0${seq}.000Z`,\n47\t  actor: role === \"user\" ? { type: \"human\", id: \"smoke@turnbridge.test\" } : { type: \"agent\", id: \"probe-model\" },\n48\t  producer: { tool: \"turnbridge-smoke\", source: \"claude-code\", session_id: conversationId.split(\":\")[1] },\n49\t  conversation: { id: conversationId, seq },\n50\t  content: { role, blocks: [{ type: \"text\", text }] },\n51\t});\n52\tawait appendEvents(repo, [\n53\t  turn(\"user\", `The secret marker phrase is ${MARKER}. Remember it.`, 0),\n54\t  turn(\"assistant\", `Noted. I will recall the marker phrase when asked.`, 1),\n55\t]);\n56\tconst summary = (await listConversations(repo, { all: true })).find((c) => c.id === conversationId);\n57\tconst plan = await targetFor(\"codex\").fabricate(summary, dir);\n58\tconst sessionId = plan.fabricatedConversationId.split(\":\")[1];\n59\tconst rolloutPath = plan.notes.find((n) => n.includes(\"rollout file:\")).split(\"rollout file: \")[1];\n60\tconsole.log(`fabricated session ${sessionId}`);\n61\t\n62\t// 3. Inject the donor reasoning item immediately before the final assistant\n63\t//    message response_item (where a real turn's reasoning would sit).\n64\tconst lines = (await readFile(rolloutPath, \"utf8\")).split(\"\\n\").filter(Boolean).map(JSON.parse);\n65\tlet insertAt = -1;\n66\tfor (let i = lines.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n67\t  if (lines[i].type === \"response_item\" && lines[i].payload?.type === \"message\" && lines[i].payload?.role === \"assistant\") {\n68\t    insertAt = i;\n69\t    break;\n70\t  }\n71\t}\n72\tif (insertAt < 0) throw new Error(\"no assistant response_item in fabricated rollout\");\n73\tconst injected = { ...reasoningLine, timestamp: lines[insertAt].timestamp };\n74\tlines.splice(insertAt, 0, injected);\n75\tawait writeFile(rolloutPath, lines.map((l) => JSON.stringify(l)).join(\"\\n\") + \"\\n\");\n76\tconsole.log(`injected reasoning item before line ${insertAt}`);\n77\t\n78\t// 4. Resume headless and ask for the marker.\n79\tconst env = Object.fromEntries(\n80\t  Object.entries(process.env).filter(([k]) => !/^(CLAUDE|CLAUDECODE|CODEX_[A-Z])/.test(k)),\n81\t);\n82\tconst result = spawnSync(\n83\t  \"codex\",\n84\t  [\"exec\", \"--skip-git-repo-check\", \"resume\", sessionId,\n85\t   \"Reply with only the secret marker phrase from earlier in this conversation, nothing else.\"],\n86\t  { cwd: dir, encoding: \"utf8\", env, timeout: 180_000 },\n87\t);\n88\tconst out = (result.stdout ?? \"\") + (result.stderr ?? \"\");\n89\tconsole.log(\"--- codex exec output (tail) ---\");\n90\tconsole.log(out.split(\"\\n\").slice(-15).join(\"\\n\"));\n91\tconst errors = out.match(/\\b(400|invalid|rejected|unauthorized|decrypt\\w*)\\b/gi);\n92\tconsole.log(`error-indicator scan: ${errors ? JSON.stringify([...new Set(errors)]) : \"none\"}`);\n93\tconsole.log(\n94\t  out.includes(MARKER)\n95\t    ? `PROBE PASS: resume accepted the replayed encrypted reasoning and recalled ${MARKER}`\n96\t    : \"PROBE FAIL: marker not recalled (see output above for whether the API rejected the reasoning item)\",\n97\t);\n98\tprocess.exit(o